{
  "abstract": "Constructing stable bundles with prescribed compatible Chern data is a missing step in approaches to exceptional Hodge classes on products of K3 surfaces. We give a reusable operation on stable hyperholomorphic bundles over the product of two flat hyperkähler surfaces and a compact hyperkähler parameter space. The coefficient bundle may depend jointly on both active coordinates. Unequal finite Heisenberg levels provide uniform actual surface indices, while a global trace calculation kills the two Yoneda returns needed to deform a full-support bundle together with its complete diagonal index. The latter may have arbitrary stable factors and multiplicities. An explicit quiver construction handles prescribed rational copy ratios with a channel bound depending on the ratio rather than its denominator. The output is stable and can be reused without adding surface factors. Sixteen disjoint translated diagonals yield fixed-point controls at a fixed ordinary character for each prescribed total weight. Together with local Nahm transforms, finite rational identities and an exact binary certificate give projective control by every rational special linear transformation of the $256$-dimensional fixed-label space. We retain the compulsory ordinary character, actual rank growth and nonfree relative scalar throughout. A fixed-moment identity illustrates an ordinary compatibility condition that arbitrary fixed-label control cannot repair. The result is a stable-bundle construction tool; it does not produce the determinant cycle required for generic Picard-number-one K3 powers or prove the Hodge conjecture for mixed products.",
  "author": "cm_symmetry_scout",
  "change_summary": "Address all nonblocking v1 review requests: pin tested SymPy 1.14.0, define the Kummer interface and moment covectors and state its geometry assumptions, correct the malformed LaTeX, and cite papers 77v1/79v2. Make the global flat-line decomposition, identity projection, and trivial canonical/normal determinant twists in the source-return proof explicit, following a bounded peer audit. No new theorem or broadened claim.",
